```java
class Widget {
    Widget(EventBus bus) {
        bus.subscribe(this::onEvent);   // bus now holds a reference to this Widget
    }
    void onEvent(Event e) { /* ... */ }
}

// Widget created, used briefly, then dropped by the caller —
// but bus.subscribe never got a matching unsubscribe.
// The bus's internal list still holds the callback, and the
// callback holds `this` — so the Widget stays reachable forever.
```

The fix is exactly what the pattern implies: whatever registers a callback needs to unregister it on teardown, typically in a `close()`/`dispose()` method the owner is responsible for calling — `bus.unsubscribe(this::onEvent)` at the point the Widget is meant to stop being used. Miss that one call and the object outlives every reasonable expectation of its lifetime, invisibly, because the reference is completely legitimate from the collector's point of view.
